# Water Cycle

These are worked answers rather than a marking scheme. Where a question asks you to reason or to
argue, what follows is one good answer and not the only one, and you should expect your own wording
to differ. Where a question asks you to compute something, the numbers below are the numbers, and
if yours disagree it is worth finding out why before reading on.

## 1. The hydrological cycle across scales

The **hydrological cycle** is the continuous movement of water between the atmosphere, the land
surface and the subsurface, transferring water between phases and between reservoirs. At the global
scale it is driven by evaporation from the oceans and land, by condensation and precipitation of
atmospheric moisture, and by the run-off and infiltration that return water to the sea. At the
catchment scale the same three processes operate, but each subdivides. Evaporation becomes open
water evaporation, soil evaporation, interception loss and transpiration; precipitation may be
intercepted by a canopy before it reaches the ground; and run-off separates into overland flow,
throughflow and groundwater flow. What changes between the two scales is not the physics but the
level of detail at which we are obliged to describe it.

Taking three processes in turn, evaporation and transpiration return water to the atmosphere, and
while the ocean dominates globally, it is vegetation and soil moisture that set the rate within a
catchment. Precipitation delivers water, and where atmospheric circulation governs its global
pattern, topography and storm tracks govern the local one. Run-off and infiltration partition water
at the surface, and this is almost entirely a catchment-scale process, yet it is what closes the
global cycle. The three are coupled: water that infiltrates today is water that evaporates or
discharges later, so a change in one term propagates through the others.

On the simplifications, several are defensible. The global diagram is a **steady-state** picture,
so it conceals year-to-year variability and any long-term trend. It treats each reservoir as
uniform and well mixed. It omits human intervention entirely, and there are no dams, irrigation
schemes or abstraction wells anywhere in it. It reports annual totals, so seasonality vanishes.
It draws groundwater as a single flux, when in reality groundwater spans residence times from days
to millennia.

Whether these matter to a catchment hydrologist depends on which one you pick, and the steady-state
and annual-average assumptions matter a great deal. A catchment hydrologist works with storms,
seasons and droughts, and the diagram says nothing about any of them. The well-mixed reservoir
assumption is more forgiving for questions of water quantity, but it becomes serious as soon as
water quality is at stake, since a contaminant does not distribute itself evenly through an aquifer
simply because our diagram draws the aquifer as one box.

## 2. Does the global water cycle balance?

**1. Net ocean loss and net land gain.**

$ 502{,}800 - 458{,}000 = 44{,}800 $ km³/yr is lost from the ocean, while

$ 110{,}000 - 65{,}200 = 44{,}800 $ km³/yr is gained over the land.

They are identical. The ocean loses precisely what the land gains.

**2. Return flow.**

$ 42{,}600 + 2{,}200 = 44{,}800 $ km³/yr

The budget closes exactly, with no residual at all. It had to, because the diagram represents a
closed system in steady state. Water is neither created nor destroyed, and if the stores are not
changing then everything leaving one reservoir must arrive at another. What we have just verified
is the water balance equation, $ P \pm E \pm \Delta S \pm Q = 0 $, drawn as a picture.

**3. Global totals.**

Precipitation totals $ 458{,}000 + 110{,}000 + 9{,}000 = 577{,}000 $ km³/yr, and evaporation totals
$ 502{,}800 + 65{,}200 + 9{,}000 = 577{,}000 $ km³/yr. They are equal, for the same reason as
before. If you excluded the lake terms from both sides you would have obtained 568,000 km³/yr on
each side, which is equally correct; what matters is that you treated the two sides consistently.

**4. Mean land precipitation depth.**

$ \frac{110{,}000 \text{ km}^3\text{/yr}}{149 \times 10^6 \text{ km}^2}
= \frac{1.1 \times 10^{14}\ \text{m}^3\text{/yr}}{1.49 \times 10^{14}\ \text{m}^2}
= 0.738\ \text{m/yr} \approx 740\ \text{mm/yr} $

To judge plausibility, compare it against something you already know. Commonly cited values for
mean precipitation over land fall between roughly 700 and 800 mm/yr, so we are in the right place.
It is also a little below the Australian average, which is what we should expect, given how much of
the global land surface is desert.

**5. The area labels.**

Adding them gives $ 119 + 119 + 361 = 599 \times 10^6 $ km², against an actual Earth surface area of
$ 510 \times 10^6 $ km². The figure over-counts by 89 million km², which is not a rounding problem.

Since the ocean area of 361 and the total of 510 are both well established, the land area must be
$ 510 - 361 = 149 \times 10^6 $ km², and the two runoff areas therefore cannot both be 119. The
internal, or endorheic, runoff area is the one to distrust. Internally draining basins are usually
put at roughly 20 to 30 million km², and $ 119 + 30 = 149 $ closes correctly. The label appears to
have been duplicated somewhere in the reproduction of the figure.

This does not affect parts 1 to 3, and you can say so with confidence rather than hoping, because
those parts use only the fluxes and never touch the areas. It would have affected part 4 had you
taken the land area from the figure instead of using the standard value. The wider lesson is that a
published figure can be entirely authoritative about one thing while being wrong about another, so
it is worth checking whichever part of it you intend to lean on.

<<<<

## 3. How long does water stay put?

**1. Mean residence times**, computed as $ \tau = S/Q $:

.align{center}
    | Store | $ S $ (km³) | $ Q $ (km³/yr) | $ \tau $ |
    |:------|------------:|---------------:|---------:|
    | Atmosphere  | $ 1.29 \times 10^4 $ | 577,000 (global precipitation) | 0.0224 yr, about **8 days** |
    | Rivers      | $ 2.12 \times 10^3 $ | 42,600 (river run-off) | 0.0498 yr, about **18 days** |
    | Oceans      | $ 1.338 \times 10^9 $ | 502,800 (ocean evaporation) | about **2,700 years** |
    | Groundwater | $ 2.34 \times 10^7 $ | 2,200 (groundwater flow to ocean) | about **10,600 years** |
    "Mean residence times of the major global stores."

Taking the atmosphere as a worked example, $ \tau = 12{,}900 / 577{,}000 = 0.02236 $ yr, and
multiplying by 365.25 gives 8.2 days.

One honest caveat on the groundwater figure. We divided by the flux to the ocean, which is an
underestimate of the total throughput, since groundwater is also lost to evapotranspiration and to
river baseflow. Using total recharge instead would give a shorter residence time. Either is
defensible provided you say which flux you used, and the order of magnitude, being thousands to
tens of thousands of years, is the part of the answer that survives the choice.

**2. Reconciling the two statements.** Residence time is a ratio, so a store can be very small and
still pass enormous volumes if it turns over quickly. The atmosphere holds only 12,900 km³, yet it
cycles 577,000 km³ every year, which is roughly forty-five times its own volume. Every raindrop
must pass through it, and none of them stay long. The useful lesson is that the size of a store
tells us nothing on its own about its importance, and we must always ask how fast it turns over.

**3. Ice caps.** You cannot compute it, and the reason is that Figure 1.7 gives no flux term for ice
at all. There is no accumulation, no melt and no calving anywhere in the diagram. To proceed you
would need an independent estimate of snowfall accumulation or of total mass loss. Published values
range from decades for small alpine glaciers to more than $ 10^5 $ years for the interior of the
Antarctic ice sheet, which is itself worth noticing: "ice" is not one store but many, with
turnover times that differ by four orders of magnitude.

**4. The two spills.** A river flushes itself in about eighteen days, so a spill is diluted and
exported quickly. The damage is acute and extends a long way downstream, but it is short lived, and
remediation consists largely of stopping the source and waiting. An aquifer turns over on a scale
of thousands of years, so contamination is permanent on any timescale we care about. It does not
flush, it migrates slowly and unpredictably, it is expensive even to monitor, and active
remediation by pumping and treating may run for decades without finishing. The same spill has
produced two entirely different problems, and the difference is residence time.

**5. Why long residence time means non-renewable.** Belonging to a cycle guarantees that water is
eventually replaced, but it says nothing whatever about how fast. If an aquifer is replenished on a
ten-thousand-year timescale and we abstract from it annually, we are removing water some thousands
of times faster than it returns. That is mining a resource rather than harvesting it, and the fact
that the water is technically part of a renewable cycle offers no comfort at all. The comparison
that matters is always the rate of use against the rate of renewal, and never the volume in store.

<<<<

## 4. A number worth checking

**1. Reproducing the calculation.**

$ 0.27\% \times 1{,}385{,}984 \times 10^3\ \text{km}^3 = 3.742 \times 10^6\ \text{km}^3 $

$ 3.742 \times 10^6\ \text{km}^3 \times 10^{12}\ \text{L/km}^3 = 3.742 \times 10^{18}\ \text{L} $

$ \frac{3.742 \times 10^{18}}{7 \times 10^9} = 5.35 \times 10^8\ \text{L} $, which is
**535 million litres per person**.

**2. The size of the disagreement.** Since $ 535 / 146 = 3.66 $, the two figures differ by a factor
of about 3.7.

Working backwards, 146 million litres for seven billion people requires
$ 146 \times 10^6 \times 7 \times 10^9 = 1.022 \times 10^{18} $ L, which is
$ 1.022 \times 10^6 $ km³, or **0.074%** of the total rather than 0.27%. The two numbers printed in
the textbook therefore cannot both follow from the same population, and the text does not tell us
which one to keep.

**3. The units, which is the important part.** Neither "per day" nor "per year" can be correct.
Taking 0.27% of a store yields a volume, in litres per person, and that is the end of it. The
quantity has no time dimension, because nothing in the calculation introduced one. To turn a store
into a legitimate per-year figure we would have to divide by a residence time, or otherwise specify
a rate of renewal, and no such step is taken anywhere. Both time labels are therefore artefacts,
and the fact that the book uses different ones on facing pages is the clue that something has gone
astray.

This is the store and flux distinction from Question 3, arriving in a place where it does real
damage. A store tells us how much water exists. Only a flux tells us how much we may use.

**4. An honest figure.**

$ \frac{42{,}600\ \text{km}^3\text{/yr}}{8 \times 10^9}
= \frac{4.26 \times 10^{13}\ \text{m}^3\text{/yr}}{8 \times 10^9}
= 5{,}325\ \text{m}^3 $ per person per year

That is about 5.3 million litres per person per year, some 27 times smaller than the published
headline and about a hundred times smaller than the 535 million litres implied by the 0.27% share.

**5. Placing it in the table.** Our figure of 5,325 m³/cap/yr is $ 5.3 \times 10^3 $ m³/cap/yr,
which sits between the United Kingdom at 2.262 and the United States at 8.914, and well below
Australia at 21.272. That is exactly where a global average belongs, below the sparsely populated
and water-rich countries and above the crowded or arid ones, and the fact that it lands there is
good evidence that we have not made an error.

The 146 million litre figure converts to 146,000 m³/cap/yr, which would make the global average
richer in water than every country in the table except Iceland. That is plainly impossible, and it
is the kind of sanity check worth running on any per-capita number before quoting it. Even with the
units repaired the comparison would remain meaningless, because the table reports a renewable annual
flux, being the rainfall falling within a country's borders, while the 146 million figure is a share
of a static store. The two are different kinds of quantity and cannot be placed on the same axis.

<<<<

## 5. A year in the life of an inland catchment

**1. Monthly evaporation.** Rearranging $ P \pm E \pm \Delta S \pm Q = 0 $, with all terms taken as
positive when they represent a gain to the catchment, gives

$ E = P - Q - \Delta S $

- **January**: $ E = 28 - 1 - (-38) = 28 - 1 + 38 = 65 $ mm
- **April**: $ E = 38 - 2 - (-8) = 38 - 2 + 8 = 44 $ mm
- **July**: $ E = 55 - 4 - (+18) = 33 $ mm

The sign is where this question is won or lost. When storage falls, $ \Delta S $ is negative,
subtracting it adds to the total, and that water becomes available for evaporation. Evaporation
therefore exceeds $ P - Q $ in every month of the drawdown.

.align{center}
    | Month | $ P $ | $ Q $ | $ \Delta S $ | $ E $ |
    |:------|------:|------:|-------------:|------:|
    | Jul | 55 | 4 | +18 | 33 |
    | Aug | 52 | 5 | +14 | 33 |
    | Sep | 45 | 5 | +2  | 38 |
    | Oct | 42 | 4 | -12 | 50 |
    | Nov | 38 | 3 | -22 | 57 |
    | Dec | 30 | 2 | -34 | 62 |
    | Jan | 28 | 1 | -38 | 65 |
    | Feb | 30 | 1 | -33 | 62 |
    | Mar | 33 | 1 | -22 | 54 |
    | Apr | 38 | 2 | -8  | 44 |
    | May | 50 | 3 | +12 | 35 |
    | Jun | 59 | 4 | +23 | 32 |
    | **Total** | **500** | **35** | **-100** | **565** |
    "Completed monthly water balance, all values in mm."

**2. Annual totals.** $ P = 500 $ mm, $ Q = 35 $ mm, $ \Delta S = -100 $ mm and $ E = 565 $ mm.

Checking, $ 500 - 35 - (-100) = 565 $ mm, as required.

**3. Can evaporation exceed precipitation?** It can, and there is nothing paradoxical about it.
Over this year the catchment evaporated 565 mm while receiving only 500 mm. The additional 65 mm,
together with the 35 mm that ran off, came out of storage, which fell by 100 mm across the year.
The catchment spent its savings, and that is the answer to what kind of year this was: a drought.

It cannot do so indefinitely. Storage is finite, and a run of years like this one draws down soil
moisture and groundwater until either the store is exhausted or evaporation becomes water-limited
and falls of its own accord. Averaged over a long enough record $ \Delta S $ tends to zero, and
$ E $ must then be less than $ P $. A single year in which $ E > P $ is the signature of a drought,
not of a measurement error, and recognising that distinction is the point of the question.

**4. Run-off coefficient.**

$ \frac{Q}{P} = \frac{35}{500} = 0.07 $, or 7%

Against roughly one third in a humid temperate catchment, this is strikingly low, and the reasons
are the defining features of a semi-arid water balance. High potential evaporation and low humidity
return most rainfall to the atmosphere before it can reach a channel. Rainfall arrives in modest
amounts spread through the year rather than concentrated in a wet season, so intensities rarely
exceed the infiltration capacity and infiltration-excess overland flow is uncommon. Soils are dry
and the unsaturated zone is deep, so saturation-excess flow is rarer still. Sparse vegetation and
extensive bare soil intercept and store water on its way down. Between them these mechanisms
consume almost all of the precipitation, and only a small remainder ever becomes stream flow.

**5. From depth to volume and discharge.**

$ A = 5{,}000\ \text{km}^2 = 5 \times 10^9\ \text{m}^2 $ and $ d = 35\ \text{mm} = 0.035\ \text{m} $

$ V = d \times A = 0.035 \times 5 \times 10^9 = 1.75 \times 10^8\ \text{m}^3 $

$ V = \frac{1.75 \times 10^8}{10^6} = \mathbf{175\ GL} $

Taking a year as $ 3.156 \times 10^7 $ s, the mean discharge is

$ \bar{Q} = \frac{1.75 \times 10^8\ \text{m}^3}{3.156 \times 10^7\ \text{s}} \approx \mathbf{5.5\ m^3/s} $

**6. The storage loss.**

$ 0.100\ \text{m} \times 5 \times 10^9\ \text{m}^2 = 5 \times 10^8\ \text{m}^3 = \mathbf{500\ GL} $

The catchment lost 500 GL from storage in a single year, which is nearly three times its entire
annual run-off. What "recovery" means depends entirely on which store the water came from. Soil
moisture will recover within months of good rain. If a substantial part of the loss was
groundwater, then with a residence time on the order of $ 10^3 $ to $ 10^4 $ years, recovery is not
something that happens over a wet season or even over a wet decade, and on any timescale relevant
to the people farming this catchment it does not happen at all. This is the difference between a
store being drawn upon and a store being mined, and it is precisely what a single lumped
$ \Delta S $ term conceals.

<<<<

## 6. Magnitude, frequency and duration

**Part A. The flow record.**

**1. Why the mean sits above the median.** The distribution is positively skewed, with a great many
low flows and a long tail of rare high ones. The mean is sensitive to those extreme values and is
dragged upward by them, whereas the median, being a rank statistic, is not. A normal distribution
would be symmetric, with mean and median coinciding. Hydrological variables are almost never
normally distributed, and this is why so much of hydrological statistics works with transformed
variables or with extreme-value distributions instead.

**2. Exceedance probability.** With $ N = 38 \times 365.25 \approx 13{,}880 $ days and $ n = 7 $,

$ p = \frac{n}{N} = \frac{7}{13{,}880} = 5.0 \times 10^{-4} $, or **0.05%**

which is the figure quoted in the textbook.

**3. Which statistic to quote.** For the typical flow, give the median of 0.25 m³/s. It describes
what the river is actually doing on an ordinary day, and half the record lies on either side of it.
The mean of 0.55 m³/s is exceeded only about a third of the time, so it is not typical of anything.

For sizing a bridge neither statistic is any use, and you need the extreme tail instead, in the form
of a design flood at a chosen recurrence interval obtained from flood frequency analysis. A bridge
that survives the median flow is a bridge that fails.

**Part B. The design rainfall curves.**

**4. Depth and intensity at a 20-year recurrence interval.** Values within a few millimetres of
these are fine, since the point is the pattern rather than the precision.

.align{center}
    | Duration | Depth (mm) | Mean intensity (mm/h) |
    |:---------|-----------:|----------------------:|
    | 1 h  | about 23 | 23  |
    | 6 h  | about 45 | 7.5 |
    | 24 h | about 72 | 3.0 |
    | 48 h | about 92 | 1.9 |
    "Depth and intensity against duration for a 20-year event."

**5. Why both trends occur.** Depth rises with duration for the simple reason that rain continues to
fall, so a 48-hour storm accumulates more water than a one-hour burst and must deliver more in order
to be equally rare. Intensity falls because the atmospheric conditions that produce very high
rainfall rates, namely intense convection, strong uplift and abundant moisture convergence, cannot
be sustained. A convective cell can deliver 23 mm/h for an hour, but nothing delivers 23 mm/h for
two days. Long-duration events are built from moderate rainfall sustained by large frontal or
synoptic systems, not from extreme rates maintained for a long time.

**6. Choosing a duration.** For an urban stormwater drain, use a short duration of an hour or less.
Urban catchments are small, sealed and steep, with times of concentration measured in minutes, so
the peak flow is set by the most intense short burst and a long-duration average would badly
under-design the pipe.

For a flood levee on a large river, use a long duration of 24 to 48 hours or more. A large catchment
integrates rainfall over a long travel time, so a brief intense cell over one sub-catchment barely
registers at the outlet, and what generates the peak is sustained, widespread rainfall across the
whole basin.

The principle connecting the two is to match the storm duration to the catchment's time of
concentration.

**Part C. Recurrence intervals.**

**7. Why the resident is wrong.** The recurrence interval $ T = 1/p $ describes a long-run average
frequency and not a schedule. A one-in-a-hundred-year flood has $ p = 0.01 $ of occurring in any
year, including the one immediately following such a flood. The river retains no memory of what it
did last year, and successive years are, to a good approximation, independent. The argument is the
gambler's fallacy applied to a floodplain, and it is not merely an academic error, since it has
encouraged real people to rebuild in places where they should not have.

There is one genuine caveat worth adding. If antecedent conditions remain wet, or if the climate is
non-stationary, the risk in the following year may in fact be higher rather than lower.

**8. Probability over thirty years.** The probability of no flood in a given year is
$ 1 - 0.01 = 0.99 $, so across thirty independent years

$ P(\text{none}) = 0.99^{30} = 0.7397 $, and $ P(\text{at least one}) = 1 - 0.7397 \approx \mathbf{26\%} $

Roughly a one-in-four chance over the life of a mortgage. Most people's intuition for a
"one in a hundred year" event puts the figure very much lower, which is part of why floodplain
development remains such a persistent problem.

**9. On terminology.** The phrase "return period" invites precisely the misreading in part 7,
because it sounds as though the event returns on a fixed cycle. "Recurrence interval" makes it
clearer that the number is an average spacing between events of a given magnitude taken over a long
record, and that it carries no implication whatever about when the next one will arrive.

<<<<

## 7. Delineating a catchment boundary

**1 and 2. A workable procedure.** Any procedure equivalent to the following will do, and since the
point of the exercise was to derive it, the reasoning matters more than the wording.

1. Identify the **outlet**, or pour point, on the stream, this being the point whose contributing
   area we want.
2. Identify the **stream network** and the valleys it occupies. Contours form a V pointing upstream
   where they cross a valley, and a rounded nose pointing downhill where they cross a ridge.
3. Mark the **high points and ridgelines** surrounding the stream network, since these are the
   divides.
4. Trace the boundary along those ridgelines, **crossing contours at right angles**, working
   outwards from one side of the outlet, around the head of the catchment, and back down the other.
5. **Close the loop** at the outlet, then check connectivity: everything inside must drain to the
   outlet, and everything outside must drain elsewhere.

!(50%)[topomap](../image/tutorial_usgs_catchment_answer.png "The delineated catchment boundary for the marked outlet.")

Applied to this map, the outlet sits near the centre bottom of the figure, where the stream leaves
the valley. Flow lines run downhill perpendicular to the contours, and the valleys are marked by
contours forming V shapes that point upstream. The northern and western ridgelines show closely
spaced contours curving outward, and that is the high ground. The boundary follows them, running up
from the outlet along the eastern ridge, around the head of the catchment to the north, and back
down the western slope to close at the outlet.

**3. Comparing procedures.** The differences that tend to be genuine are these: whether you began at
the outlet or at the ridges, whether you drew flow lines explicitly before locating the divide or
inferred the divide directly, and whether you treated the stream network as given or derived it
from the contours first. All three approaches can be made to work. The differences that are not
genuine are differences of vocabulary, since "watershed", "divide" and "catchment boundary" all
name the same line.

**4. Physical justification.** Flow lines cross contours at right angles because water follows the
steepest descent, and the direction of steepest descent on a surface is perpendicular to the
contour. The contour is by definition the direction along which elevation does not change, so the
gradient must be normal to it.

The boundary follows high points and closes at the outlet because a divide is the locus from which
water drains in two different directions. It cannot run along a valley, since water there is
already committed to one catchment or the other. It must close on itself because the catchment is a
finite, bounded area, and a boundary left open would leave the catchment undefined.

**5. Where confidence fails.** Flat terrain is the usual difficulty, because contours are widely
spaced and a small error in reading their position becomes a large error in the divide. Broad
saddles and plateaux are similar, and there the position of the divide may be genuinely ambiguous
rather than merely hard to see. Karst and fractured rock present a different problem, since surface
topography may not control the flow at all. Modified landscapes present a third, because roads,
drains, culverts and irrigation channels routinely route water straight across a topographic
divide.

**6. Surface and groundwater divides.** Your sketch should show a hill in cross section with the
surface water divide at the topographic high, and the groundwater divide displaced to one side. The
reason is that the water table is a subdued replica of the topography whose shape is controlled by
geology, so a permeable layer dipping over an impermeable one can carry groundwater beneath the
surface divide and into the neighbouring catchment. Figure 1.6 in the textbook shows this.

The practical consequence is that a catchment water balance may fail to close if it is computed on
the topographic boundary alone, because water is entering or leaving unseen. Allocating water on
the basis of surface catchments can therefore over-allocate one basin while quietly starving its
neighbour, which is why integrated water resource management works with both boundaries at once.

**7. Manual against digital.** Manual delineation is slow, subjective and unreliable in flat or
complex terrain. What it has in its favour is that it obliges you to look at the landscape, and
that its output is easy to sanity-check.

Digital, DEM-based delineation is fast, reproducible and scalable, and it brings its own problems.
The result is only as good as the resolution and vertical accuracy of the underlying elevation
model. Sink filling deliberately alters the real topography in order to make the algorithm work.
The D8 algorithm forces flow into one of eight directions, which produces unrealistic parallel
straight-line paths across planar hillslopes and performs poorly on flat ground. The approach still
assumes that topography controls flow, so it inherits every limitation discussed in part 6. Above
all, a digital result looks authoritative, and for that reason it is rarely checked.

## 8. Water, climate and scarcity

The following sketches the ground a strong answer would cover. These are open questions, so a good
essay may take a different route through the material.

**(a) Physical properties and climate.** The most useful thing to notice is that these are not four
independent properties but four consequences of one molecular cause. Hydrogen bonding is the root
of all of them, and it is why water requires so much energy to change either its temperature or its
phase. Its specific heat capacity of 4.2 kJ/kg/K, against 1.1 for dry soil and 0.44 for iron, means
that oceans and lakes warm and cool slowly, which is why maritime climates have milder summers and
winters than continental ones at the same latitude. Latent heat is the largest effect of the four,
since something like 70% of the Earth's lateral energy transport is carried as latent heat in water
vapour moving from the equator towards the poles; without it the equator-to-pole temperature
gradient would be far steeper than it is. Water vapour is also the dominant greenhouse gas. The
density anomaly, with maximum density at 3.98 °C, means that ice floats and lakes freeze from the
top down, insulating the water beneath, and had ice sunk instead, water bodies would freeze solid
from the bottom and aquatic life could not survive a winter.

**(b) The Stumm statement.** The statement is arithmetically defensible and practically useless, and
the reason is the distinction between stores and fluxes. The total quantity of fresh water is
indeed large, but a quantity held in store is not a supply, and what can actually be used is the
renewable flux, which amounts to roughly 5,300 m³ per person per year of river run-off rather than
the far larger store-based figures. On spatial inequality, Iceland holds 525,000 m³/cap/yr against
essentially nothing in Kuwait, and Australia appears water-rich at 21,272 m³/cap/yr only because
high rainfall in the tropical north-west conceals severe scarcity across the interior and the
Murray-Darling Basin. Temporal inequality matters as much as spatial, as the 2005 to 2007 drought
demonstrated. The measure itself is flawed, being based on rainfall falling within national borders
and therefore ignoring transboundary rivers, shared aquifers, and the virtual water embedded in
trade. Hydrology contributes as a pure science by quantifying and explaining these disparities, and
as an applied science through the design of abstraction and storage, through allocation policy, and
through the remote sensing of water resources that occupies the second half of this course.

**(c) Overcoming scarcity.** The available strategies include increasing storage to buffer temporal
variability, whether through dams or managed aquifer recharge; transferring water between basins;
desalination, treated honestly, which means acknowledging its energy cost; demand management and
irrigation efficiency, which is where most water actually goes and therefore where most of it can
be saved; and treaties governing shared rivers and aquifers. To these should be added virtual
water, meaning the import of water-intensive goods in place of the water itself, which is how
several of the water-poorest countries in Table 1.3 function in practice. The strongest answers
note that every one of these strategies moves the problem somewhere else rather than dissolving it,
whether onto the energy system, onto an upstream neighbour, or onto the groundwater of an exporting
country. Dalin et al. (2017) found that around 11% of non-renewable groundwater abstraction for
irrigation is embedded in international food trade, which is the same displacement seen from the
other end.

