## Extra: digital delineation using the D8 method

Digital watershed delineation is normally performed on a **Digital Elevation Model**, which is a
grid of elevation values. The most widely used flow-direction algorithm is **D8**, standing for
Deterministic Eight. This section is not examinable, but the method underlies almost every
catchment boundary you will meet in practice, and it is worth knowing what the software is doing.

### Procedure

1. **Fill sinks.** Elevation models contain local depressions, or pits, that interrupt continuous
   downslope flow, and these are filled by raising the sink to the elevation of its lowest spillover
   point. It is worth being clear that this deliberately alters the terrain, since real closed
   depressions do exist and filling them is a modelling convenience rather than a correction.

2. **Compute flow direction.** For each cell, compare its elevation with that of its eight
   neighbours, and assign flow to the direction of steepest descent, measured as the maximum drop
   per unit distance. Each cell drains to exactly one neighbour, which is what makes the method
   deterministic.

3. **Compute flow accumulation.** Beginning from headwater cells that receive no inflow, accumulate
   the number of upstream cells draining through each cell. Applying a threshold to the resulting
   grid identifies the stream network.

4. **Select an outlet**, usually snapping it to a cell of high accumulation so that it sits on the
   modelled channel rather than beside it.

5. **Delineate** by tracing upstream from the outlet through the flow-direction grid. Every cell
   that drains to the outlet belongs to the catchment.

### Flow direction encoding

Each cell stores a power-of-two code giving its outflow direction:

.align{center}
    | Direction | E | SE | S | SW | W | NW | N | NE |
    |:----------|--:|---:|--:|---:|--:|---:|--:|---:|
    | Code      | 1 | 2  | 4 | 8  | 16| 32 | 64| 128|
    "Standard D8 direction encoding."

### Limitations and tools

D8 is simple and fast, but forcing all flow into one of eight directions produces artificial
parallel flow paths across planar hillslopes and behaves poorly on flat ground. Multiple
flow-direction algorithms such as D-infinity distribute flow among several downslope neighbours and
represent divergent hillslope flow more realistically, at greater computational cost.

In Python, `pysheds` ([GitHub](https://github.com/mdbartos/pysheds)) implements the whole workflow,
and `richdem`, `numpy` and `scipy.ndimage` are useful for manipulating elevation models directly.
QGIS and ArcGIS both provide built-in watershed delineation tools.
